Say whether a daemon claimed its service, and when a listener throws a message away.

TWO SILENCES, both reported only through xpc_log_error or xpc_log_fault, which go to syslog -- and
there is no syslog socket here.

bootstrap_check_in in activateLocked is where a named XPC listener claims its service. Until that
succeeds launchd keeps the receive right, and a client that looks the name up still gets a send
right, to a queue nobody is reading. The client then waits with no timeout and no error.

And a listener DROPS any message that is not a check-in. A client whose first message is not one
waits forever while the server silently throws it away, which from outside is indistinguishable
from the daemon not running.

WHAT THE FIRST ONE ANSWERED, and it corrected a reading of mine. launchd's port-set dump shows
com.apple.trustd as recv=1 active=0, meaning launchd still holds the receive right, and I took that
to mean trustd never checks in. It does:

    CIDER_XPC check-in for service com.apple.trustd = 0 (claimed)

The port-set snapshot is taken at DEMAND DISPATCH time, before the job it just launched has had a
chance to check in, so it could never have shown anything else. A snapshot proves a state at ITS
moment, not a fact about the run.

THE SECOND ONE HAS NOT FIRED YET, and that is recorded rather than claimed: in the runs so far
trustd's listener prints neither the drop nor the accept, so where its messages actually go is
still open.
--- a/src/connection.m
+++ b/src/connection.m
@@ -184,6 +184,19 @@
